<p>Polyurethane finish — PU, in showroom shorthand — is the finish you reach for when you want a kitchen or wardrobe to feel like furniture, not joinery. It is also one of the most over-sold and under-explained interior finishes in the Indian market. This guide is built to fix that.</p>
<p>We'll walk through what PU finish actually is, how it is applied, what it should and should not cost in 2026, how it compares to laminate, acrylic, duco and membrane finishes, and which homes it makes sense for. If you have ever stood in a showroom and been told "sir, this is PU only" without quite knowing what that means — start here.</p>
<h2>01 — FoundationWhat is PU finish?</h2>
<p>PU finish is short for <strong>polyurethane finish</strong> — a multi-coat liquid coating sprayed onto a primed and sanded surface to create a hard, seamless, paint-like skin. It is not paint and it is not laminate. It sits closer to automotive coating than to either: a hardened resin shell that cures chemically rather than just drying, bonded to the substrate beneath it.</p>
<h3>How polyurethane protects furniture</h3>
<p>Once cured, a PU coat is essentially a thin layer of plastic. It resists scratches, water, mild chemicals and UV light far better than melamine or traditional French polish. Because each successive coat fuses into the one below, the finish reads as a single continuous surface — no joints, no edge banding, no visible seams. This is the look people pay PU pricing to get.</p>
<h3>PU finish vs traditional polish</h3>
<p>Traditional polish — melamine, NC lacquer, or wax-based polish — sits on top of the wood as a thin film and reveals the grain. PU does the opposite: it builds a thicker, opaque surface that hides the substrate entirely. That is why PU on MDF or HDHMR looks the same as PU on plywood — you are paying for the finish, not the board underneath. Polish is for showing off wood. PU is for shaping a furniture surface to look exactly the way you want.</p>
<h3>Why premium designers reach for PU</h3>
<p>Three reasons. First, design freedom — PU can be tinted to any colour, any sheen, including custom mixes that no laminate brand will stock for a single client. Second, edges — PU wraps continuously around a shutter, so the front and the edge are the same material with no banding to peel. Third, hand-feel — a well-buffed matte PU has a soft, almost suede-like touch that laminate simply does not.</p>
<h4>In one line</h4>
<p>PU finish is a sprayed-on, chemically cured plastic coating that gives furniture a seamless, high-end painted look — at roughly two to four times the cost of premium laminate.</p>
<h2>02 — CompositionTypes of PU finish</h2>
<p>"PU finish" is a category, not a single product. When you sign off on PU, you are picking on three axes at once: chemistry, packaging and application method. Each one affects price, finish quality and durability.</p>
<h3>By chemistry: solvent-based vs water-based</h3>
<h4>Solvent-based PU</h4>
<p>The default across most Indian factories. Harder finish, deeper gloss potential, better moisture resistance. The downside is strong fumes during application and a heavier VOC load, which is why a proper spray booth is non-negotiable.</p>
<p>Higher durability Strong fumes Most kitchens</p>
<h4>Water-based PU</h4>
<p>Newer to India and growing fast. Lower VOC, milder smell, faster surface dry. Slightly softer film than solvent PU, so it scratches a touch easier — but the gap narrows every year as the chemistry improves. Best for bedrooms, kid's rooms, and homes where ventilation is limited.</p>
<p>Low VOC Mild smell Bedrooms, kids' rooms</p>
<h3>By packaging: single-component vs two-component</h3>
<h4>Single-component (1K) PU</h4>
<p>Pre-mixed, ready to spray. Easier to apply, longer pot life, cheaper. But the cured film is less hard and less chemical-resistant than 2K. Acceptable for low-traffic areas like display units and wall panels.</p>
<h4>Two-component (2K) PU</h4>
<p>Resin and hardener mixed at the time of spraying. This is what you actually want on a kitchen or a wardrobe. The chemical cross-linking that happens after mixing is what gives 2K its scratch resistance, hardness and longevity. Almost all premium PU work in India is 2K.</p>
<h3>By application method: spray, roller, brush</h3>
<p>Spray is the only application method that produces a true PU finish. Roller and brush PU exist — usually in budget projects or for retouching — but they cannot produce the seamless, dust-free surface that PU is bought for. If a vendor quotes "PU finish" at laminate prices, ask how they're applying it. The answer is almost always brush or roller, and the result is closer to glorified enamel paint than to PU.</p>
<h2>03 — AppearanceGloss levels: from super matte to mirror gloss</h2>
<p>The same PU resin, in the same colour, looks like a different material at different sheen levels. Gloss is measured on a 0–100 scale based on how much light the surface reflects at a 60-degree angle. Indian factories typically offer six tiers:</p>
<p>Super Matte0–5 GU</p>
<p>Matte5–15 GU</p>
<p>Satin15–35 GU</p>
<p>Semi Gloss35–70 GU</p>
<p>High Gloss70–90 GU</p>
<p>Mirror Gloss90+ GU</p>
<p>Best for</p>
<p><strong>Super matte / matte:</strong> daily-use kitchens, master wardrobes, dark colours</p>
<p><strong>Gloss / mirror:</strong> showroom kitchens, small spaces that need to bounce light, light colours</p>
<p>Fingerprint visibility</p>
<p>Hidden — matte forgives almost everything</p>
<p>Brutal — every smudge shows within hours</p>
<p>Ageing</p>
<p>Slow, even, classic</p>
<p>High gloss can micro-scratch and look dull in 4–5 years</p>
<p>Cost difference</p>
<p>Baseline</p>
<p>10–20% premium for high/mirror gloss</p>
<h3>Textured PU</h3>
<p>A separate category worth mentioning: textured PU adds a fine grain, suede or sandstone-like feel to the surface during the final coat. It is harder to apply, more expensive (roughly 15–25% over matte) and looks distinctly different in person than in photos. Worth specifying on one or two statement units — a tall pantry, an island front — rather than a whole kitchen.</p>
<h2>04 — PalettePU finish colours available</h2>
<p>One of the strongest arguments for PU finish is colour. Unlike laminate, which restricts you to whatever the brand decided to print this year, PU can be tinted to almost any RAL or NCS code your designer specifies — usually within a 24-hour lab match.</p>
<p>White</p>
<p>The most-ordered shade. Pure white, warm white, off-white, ivory — small undertone shifts change the whole room.</p>
<p>Grey</p>
<p>From cool dove grey to deep cement. Hides daily wear better than white. Pairs with chrome or matte black hardware.</p>
<p>Black</p>
<p>Demands matte. Reads expensive when lit well, oppressive when not. Stunning on islands and tall units.</p>
<p>Wood-shade PU</p>
<p>PU tinted to mimic walnut, oak or teak. Softer than veneer, more consistent than wood-finish laminate.</p>
<p>Statement colours</p>
<p>Forest green, navy blue, burgundy, ochre. Where PU truly wins over laminate — custom shades, perfect colour match.</p>
<p>Metallic PU</p>
<p>Champagne, gun metal, brushed bronze. Specialty tier, 20–30% premium, best in small doses.</p>
<p><strong>On custom colour matching.</strong> Most premium brands — ICA, Sirca, Milesi — will match a Pantone or RAL code with surprising accuracy. The catch is sample size: always insist on a 6"×6" sprayed sample on the actual board you're using, viewed in your home's lighting, before signing off the full job. A swatch on glossy paper will lie to you.</p>
<h2>05 — MethodHow PU finish is applied: the 10-step process</h2>
<p>Most clients never see this happen, which is exactly why PU pricing varies so wildly. A factory PU process should look like this — if your vendor skips three or more of these steps, you are paying PU prices for sub-PU quality.</p>
<h4>Surface preparation</h4>
<p>Boards are inspected, edges trimmed, and any defects (chips, knots, splinters) filled. The board's moisture content is checked — anything above 8% will cause adhesion problems later.</p>
<p>Day 1</p>
<h4>Coarse sanding</h4>
<p>An initial pass with 120-grit sandpaper to open the surface pores so primer can grip. Skipping this is the single most common cause of PU peeling later.</p>
<p>Day 1</p>
<h4>Primer coat</h4>
<p>A 2K polyurethane primer is sprayed evenly. Primer fills micro-pores in the board and creates a uniform base for the colour to sit on. Two coats minimum; three for darker shades.</p>
<p>Day 1–2</p>
<h4>Putty filling</h4>
<p>Any remaining low spots — screw holes, joinery gaps, MDF edge irregularities — are filled with PU-compatible putty. This is the step that separates a factory finish from a site finish.</p>
<p>Day 2</p>
<h4>Intermediate sanding</h4>
<p>The primed surface is sanded with 320-grit paper to level the primer and putty. Dust is blown off with compressed air. This step happens between every spray coat in a serious workshop.</p>
<p>Day 2</p>
<h4>Base coat</h4>
<p>The first tinted PU layer. Sprayed in a controlled, dust-free booth at the right viscosity. Two coats minimum, with sanding between them. By the end of this stage, the surface has its colour.</p>
<p>Day 3</p>
<h4>Inspection and touch-up</h4>
<p>Surface checked under angled light for orange peel, dust nibs, runs and pin holes. Defects are spot-sanded and re-sprayed before the final coat.</p>
<p>Day 3–4</p>
<h4>Final top coat</h4>
<p>The clear or tinted top coat is sprayed at the specified gloss level. This is the layer you actually see and touch. Done right, it lays down dead flat with no orange peel and no nibs.</p>
<p>Day 4</p>
<h4>Curing</h4>
<p>The chemistry of 2K PU finishes hardening for 48–72 hours after the final coat — and reaches full chemical hardness only after 7 days. Rushing delivery before curing completes is the second-biggest reason for PU complaints.</p>
<p>Day 5–7</p>
<h4>Final buffing</h4>
<p>Once cured, the surface is buffed to its final sheen — matte, satin or gloss — with progressively finer compounds. Only after this step should the units leave the factory.</p>
<p>Day 7</p>
<h4>What this means for you</h4>
<p>A proper PU job takes 5–7 working days per unit, minimum. If a vendor promises a fully PU-finished modular kitchen in three days, they are skipping curing — and your finish will scratch and peel within a year.</p>
<h2>06 — ApplicationWhere to use PU finish in your home</h2>
<p>PU finish makes sense anywhere you want a seamless painted look, where edges and joinery should disappear, and where moisture or daily wear demand a hard protective coat. It does not make sense everywhere — and choosing wisely is half the cost-saving.</p>
<h4>Modular kitchens</h4>
<p>The flagship use case. Shutters, tall units, island fronts, pantry doors — all classic PU territory. Recommended on the units you touch every day.</p>
<h4>Wardrobes</h4>
<p>Sliding and hinged. PU's seamless shutter face is what makes premium wardrobes feel premium. Worth the cost on master bedrooms; optional in kids' rooms.</p>
<h4>TV &amp; media units</h4>
<p>One of the most photographed surfaces in any home. PU gives the seamless, joinery-free look that built-in TV units need to feel architectural.</p>
<h4>Crockery &amp; bar units</h4>
<p>Where furniture meets display. PU gives a clean, lacquered look that flatters glass shutters and brass hardware. Common pairing with veneer back panels.</p>
<h4>Bedroom furniture</h4>
<p>Bedside tables, dressers, study units. Quieter places where PU's softness and lack of grain feels appropriate. Matte is the right choice here, almost always.</p>
<h4>Vanity units</h4>
<p>Bathroom-adjacent furniture takes constant moisture and steam. PU's water resistance beats laminate edges here — but use marine plywood underneath, not MDF.</p>
<h4>Wall panelling</h4>
<p>PU wall panels — especially in fluted or grooved profiles — are a trending alternative to wallpaper. Hard-wearing, easy to wipe, and uniform across an entire wall.</p>
<h4>Ceiling panels</h4>
<p>Decorative ceiling treatments — beams, coffers, drop panels — finished in PU for a clean architectural look. Best in living rooms and entry foyers.</p>
<h4>Shoe &amp; entry cabinets</h4>
<p>High-touch, high-traffic. PU's scratch resistance pays off here. A common upgrade from laminate, especially in apartment foyers.</p>
<h3>Where PU is usually the wrong choice</h3>
<p>Skip PU on internal carcass boxes (no one sees them, laminate is cheaper), on rental property kitchens (the budget rarely justifies the cost), and on any unit you genuinely plan to replace inside 5 years. PU's value is in its longevity — pay for it only where you'll actually keep it.</p>
<h2>07 — HonestPros and cons of PU finish</h2>
<p>Every paid guide on PU lists ten advantages and never mentions a single drawback. Here is the version we actually tell clients.</p>
<h4>What PU does well</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Seamless surface.</strong> No joints, no edge bands, no visible substrate. The shutter looks like a single piece of poured material.</li>
  <li><strong>Colour freedom.</strong> Any RAL, any NCS, any custom mix. Laminates and acrylics cannot match this flexibility.</li>
  <li><strong>Hard, scratch-resistant film</strong> once fully cured. Survives daily kitchen use better than enamel paint or melamine.</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture and stain resistance.</strong> Wipe clean, no edge-swelling, no peeling at corners.</li>
  <li><strong>UV stability</strong> in the modern formulations — premium brands no longer yellow noticeably in normal indoor light.</li>
  <li><strong>Long life.</strong> A well-applied 2K PU finish holds up for 10–12 years before it starts to look tired.</li>
  <li><strong>Premium hand-feel.</strong> A buffed matte PU surface has a softness laminate cannot replicate.</li>
</ul>
<h4>What PU doesn't tell you</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Two to four times the cost</strong> of premium laminate. Often more, depending on finish.</li>
  <li><strong>Manufacturing time is longer</strong> — 5–7 days per unit minimum, against 2–3 for laminate.</li>
  <li><strong>Repair is hard locally.</strong> A scratch or chip cannot be invisibly fixed at site; the panel usually has to go back to a spray booth.</li>
  <li><strong>Skill-dependent.</strong> Two factories with the same materials produce visibly different results. The carpenter and sprayer matter as much as the brand of PU.</li>
  <li><strong>Site PU is a downgrade.</strong> Done without a dust-free booth, the finish picks up particles and never looks factory-grade.</li>
  <li><strong>Cheap PU exists and is widely sold.</strong> Single-component, brush-applied "PU" is technically PU but produces a finish closer to glossy enamel paint.</li>
  <li><strong>Strong fumes</strong> during application — solvent PU is not something you want to be present for, especially with young children at home.</li>
</ul>
