<h3>PU finish vs laminate — the most common comparison</h3>
<p>Laminate is the default Indian kitchen surface for a reason: cost, choice and the fact that a damaged sheet can usually be swapped out without redoing the whole shutter. PU wins on appearance and feel; laminate wins on practicality and price. Our honest recommendation: laminate for the lower cabinets and tall units that take daily damage, PU for the upper-band statement surfaces — island fronts, pantry doors, the wall you see when you walk in. This hybrid approach costs 30–40% less than full PU and looks nearly identical.</p>
<h3>PU finish vs acrylic</h3>
<p>Acrylic is essentially a sheet of high-gloss plastic bonded to the board. Closer to laminate in installation, closer to PU in look. The advantages: factory-controlled gloss, predictable colour, easier to clean. The disadvantages: you cannot get matte or super-matte in acrylic (it is intrinsically glossy), and edges still need banding. Choose acrylic if you specifically want mirror gloss; choose PU if you want anything else.</p>
<h2>09 — InvestmentPU finish cost in India (2026)</h2>
<p>Cost is where the PU market gets murky. Quotes can vary by 3x for what is nominally the same finish, and most of that variance is real — different brands, different application methods, different number of coats. Here is what each price tier actually buys you.</p>
<p>Budget PU (1K, brush)</p>
<p>₹180–250</p>
<p>Standard 2K spray PU</p>
<p>₹250–380</p>
<p>Premium 2K (ICA, Asian Paints)</p>
<p>₹380–500</p>
<p>Imported PU (Sirca, Milesi)</p>
<p>₹500–650</p>
<p>Metallic / textured PU</p>
<p>₹600–800+</p>
<h3>Cost by application</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Modular kitchen (10–12 ft):</strong> ₹1.2 – 2.8 lakhs in PU alone, on top of the carcass cost. Mid-premium grade.</li>
  <li><strong>Master wardrobe (8 ft × 7 ft):</strong> ₹65,000 – 1.4 lakhs depending on finish and brand.</li>
  <li><strong>TV unit (10–12 ft wall):</strong> ₹55,000 – 1.2 lakhs for full PU; significantly less if you pair PU with veneer or back-painted glass.</li>
  <li><strong>Whole apartment (3BHK, PU on all built-ins):</strong> Add ₹4 – 8 lakhs over a comparable laminate build.</li>
</ul>
<h3>What drives the price</h3>
<p>Number of coats (premium jobs run 6–8 against budget's 3–4), brand of PU, sheen level (matte and mirror cost more than satin), colour complexity (custom RAL adds 10–15%), board material underneath, factory infrastructure, and carpenter skill. In that rough order of impact.</p>
<h2>10 — CriticalFactory PU vs site PU</h2>
<p>If there is one decision in this guide we will be loud about, it is this one. <strong>Always insist on factory PU finish.</strong> Site-applied PU is sold at the same price, looks similar at handover, and falls apart faster than almost any other finish in your home. Here is why.</p>
<p>Recommended</p>
<h4>Factory PU finish</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Dust-free spray booth — no particles trapped in the finish</li>
  <li>Climate-controlled temperature and humidity during curing</li>
  <li>Industrial spray guns deliver even, consistent coats</li>
  <li>Multiple coats with sanding between, properly cured</li>
  <li>Consistent finish across hundreds of square feet</li>
  <li>Honest warranties — usually 5–10 years on the finish</li>
  <li>Unit-by-unit quality inspection before dispatch</li>
</ul>
<p>Avoid</p>
<h4>Site PU finish</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Dust, mosquitoes, pet hair, hair from workers — all trapped in the wet finish</li>
  <li>No humidity or temperature control — finish cures unevenly</li>
  <li>Manual spray guns deliver inconsistent thickness</li>
  <li>Curing rushed to hand over the project on time</li>
  <li>Visible orange peel, runs, pin holes, dust nibs</li>
  <li>Warranties either absent or unenforceable</li>
  <li>Finish often shows defects within 6–12 months</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>The exception.</strong> Sometimes site PU is unavoidable — a built-in panelled wall that cannot be transported, a wide custom ceiling treatment, an in-place repair. In those cases, ask the vendor for: a sealed plastic tent over the work area, a HEPA-filtered air supply, and 72 hours of clear access after the final coat. It will still not match factory quality, but it can come close.</p>
<h2>11 — BrandsBest PU finish brands available in India</h2>
