<h2>12 — SubstratePU finish on different boards</h2>
<p>The board underneath your PU finish matters far more than people realise. PU is only as stable as the board it's bonded to — a warped substrate will pull the finish with it, no matter how good the spray job was.</p>
<h3>MDF — the standard for PU</h3>
<p>Medium Density Fibreboard is the most common substrate for PU finish, and for good reason: its dense, uniform surface holds primer and PU coats beautifully, with no grain to telegraph through. The downside is moisture sensitivity — standard MDF swells when exposed to water. For kitchens and vanities, demand <strong>boilo-MDF</strong> or HDHMR (see below) instead.</p>
<h3>HDHMR — the upgrade pick</h3>
<p>High-Density High-Moisture-Resistant board. Denser than MDF, holds screws better, resists moisture far longer. Costs roughly 25–35% more than MDF but is the right call for kitchens, vanities and any humid area. If your vendor quotes PU on MDF for a kitchen, ask about HDHMR — the upgrade is usually worth it.</p>
<h3>Plywood — the durable choice</h3>
<p>BWP-grade plywood gives the best long-term durability, especially in moisture-prone areas. The trade-off: ply has a visible grain that needs heavy priming and sanding to suppress, otherwise it telegraphs through the PU coat. More expensive in finish prep, but mechanically the strongest base.</p>
<h3>Veneer with PU top coat</h3>
<p>A specific combination worth its own mention: real wood veneer pasted onto plywood, then sealed with a clear PU top coat. You get the grain of real wood with the durability of PU. This is what high-end crockery units, library walls and library-style TV units are usually built from. Premium pricing, lasting result.</p>
<h3>Solid wood with PU</h3>
<p>Rare in modular contexts (too expensive, too dimensionally unstable for large shutters), but used for accent furniture — dining tables, statement chairs. PU over solid wood gives a hard protective shell while preserving the wood's grain if a clear top coat is used.</p>
<h2>13 — LifespanHow durable is PU finish — and how to care for it</h2>
<p>A well-applied 2K factory PU finish should last 10–12 years before showing its age. Some of that depends on the spray; most of it depends on how you treat it.</p>
<h3>What PU resists, and how well</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Scratches:</strong> resists everyday wear well, but a determined ceramic plate edge or a metal vessel base will leave a mark. Matte hides minor scratches; gloss reveals them.</li>
  <li><strong>Water:</strong> excellent. Standing water for hours does no damage to the film, though edges are only as good as the sealing underneath.</li>
  <li><strong>Heat:</strong> moderate. Don't place hot vessels directly on PU shutters or counters — anything above 80°C can blister the finish.</li>
  <li><strong>UV:</strong> modern formulations resist yellowing well. Older formulations and cheap PU still yellow visibly in 3–4 years, especially in white finishes near windows.</li>
  <li><strong>Chemicals:</strong> mild kitchen cleaners are fine. Acetone, paint thinner, undiluted bleach will damage the surface.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Daily and weekly care</h3>
<p>Wipe down with a soft, lint-free microfibre cloth and a few drops of mild dish soap in lukewarm water. Avoid abrasive sponges, steel wool, and any cleaner labelled "scouring" or "heavy duty." For weekly maintenance, use a dedicated furniture polish designed for painted surfaces — not wood polish, which can leave a sticky residue on PU.</p>
<h3>Common problems to watch for</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Orange peel:</strong> a bumpy texture from incorrect spray viscosity. Should never appear on a factory finish; if it does, ask for re-finish under warranty.</li>
  <li><strong>Pin holes / bubbles:</strong> caused by trapped air or moisture in the primer. Visible only at the right angle of light; usually appears in the first month.</li>
  <li><strong>Yellowing:</strong> mostly a problem with cheap or older PU formulations in white finishes. Premium 2K PU should not yellow noticeably in 5–7 years.</li>
  <li><strong>Cracking at corners:</strong> usually a substrate problem (board movement), not a PU problem. The fix is at the board level.</li>
  <li><strong>Uneven gloss:</strong> happens when the final buff was rushed. Visible as patches of different sheen on the same panel.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Quality checklist before you accept delivery</h3>
<p>01</p>
<p><strong>Uniform colour across all units.</strong> View the units side by side under the same light. Different batches should be indistinguishable.</p>
<p>02</p>
<p><strong>Smooth, dead-flat surface.</strong> Run your palm across the panel. You should feel no texture, no nibs, no orange peel.</p>
<p>03</p>
<p><strong>Crisp, fully wrapped edges.</strong> The edge should be the same colour and finish as the face, with no visible substrate or paint break.</p>
<p>04</p>
<p><strong>Reflection test.</strong> For gloss and satin finishes, look at a window's reflection in the shutter. The reflection should be sharp and undistorted.</p>
<p>05</p>
<p><strong>Touch test for hardness.</strong> Press a fingernail into a hidden area. A properly cured PU finish will not dent or mark.</p>
<p>06</p>
<p><strong>No visible joints inside the unit.</strong> Open the shutter and check the inside edges — joinery should be clean, with no PU drips into the interior.</p>
<p>07</p>
<p><strong>Door alignment.</strong> All shutters should align flush with each other when closed. Misalignment is a carpentry issue, but it shows up worse on PU than on laminate.</p>
<h2>14 — AvoidMistakes homeowners make with PU finish</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Picking PU because it sounds premium.</strong> Without understanding 1K vs 2K, factory vs site, or which brand, you are buying a category, not a finish. Read the quote, ask the questions.</li>
  <li><strong>Allowing site PU because the vendor says it's fine.</strong> It is not fine. Insist on factory finish, even if it adds two weeks to delivery.</li>
  <li><strong>Choosing high gloss for a daily-cook kitchen.</strong> The first week is glorious. By month three you'll be wiping fingerprints every evening.</li>
  <li><strong>Skipping the curing period.</strong> Accepting delivery before the 7-day cure means living with a fragile finish for the first month. Push back on rushed timelines.</li>
  <li><strong>Cheap MDF underneath premium PU.</strong> The board is half the durability story. A premium PU finish on cheap MDF will fail at the edges and joinery within two years.</li>
  <li><strong>Approving the finish from a photo or showroom sample.</strong> Always insist on a 6"×6" sprayed sample on the actual board, in the actual colour, viewed in your home's lighting.</li>
  <li><strong>Not asking about warranty.</strong> A reputable factory will offer a 5–10 year warranty on the finish itself, separate from the carpentry. If the vendor refuses, that tells you what they think of their own work.</li>
</ol>
<h2>15 — DecideIs PU finish worth the money?</h2>
<p>The honest answer depends on which home this is. Here is how we advise our own clients.</p>
<h3>If you're building a budget home (under ₹15 lakh total interior cost)</h3>
<p>Skip full PU. Use it selectively — one statement unit, maybe the island front and the pantry doors — and use premium laminate everywhere else. You'll get 80% of the visual impact for 40% of the cost.</p>
<h3>If you're building a mid-premium home (₹15–35 lakh interior cost)</h3>
<p>The sweet spot for PU. Use it on the kitchen shutters that you see when you walk in, on master wardrobe shutters, and on the main TV unit. Laminate the rest. This is where PU pays for itself in daily feel and aesthetic value.</p>
<h3>If you're building a luxury home (₹35 lakh+ interior cost)</h3>
<p>Full PU is reasonable, but consider mixing it with veneer or back-painted glass on accent surfaces. A house where every shutter is the same PU finish reads strangely uniform; mixing materials gives the interiors layered depth.</p>
<h3>If this is a rental property or a 5-year flip</h3>
<p>Don't do PU. The cost will not be recovered in rent or resale. Premium laminate looks similar enough in photographs, and the maintenance is easier for tenants.</p>
<h4>Our default recommendation</h4>
<p>For an owner-occupied home you plan to live in for ten years or more, factory 2K PU finish from a premium brand (ICA, Sirca, Milesi, or Asian Paints' top range) on the surfaces you touch and see daily — kitchen, master wardrobe, TV unit. Laminate or membrane on internal carcasses and lower-traffic units. Spend deliberately, not uniformly.</p>
